<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Morse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Samuel Finley Breese Morse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Morse was an American inventor and painter, born in Charlestown near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1791, the son of a Congregational minister. After graduating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale College</a> in 1810 he studied painting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and gained admittance to the Royal Academy, and he made his reputation as a portrait painter. In middle age he contributed to the invention of a single-wire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> system based on European telegraphs and was co-developer and namesake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morse-code">Morse code</a> in 1837. The line was officially opened on 24 May 1844, when Morse sent the words &quot;What hath God wrought&quot;, and he went on to help develop the commercial use of telegraphy. He died in 1872.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-morse.jpg" alt="Samuel Morse" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Morse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Samuel Morse</b>, painter turned inventor, was born in Charlestown.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-27">27 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
